// Stdlib: Compliant Joints + Flexures  (`std.flexures`)
//
// PRD: docs/prds/v0_3/compliant-joints-flexures.md §4.2, §11 task β.
//
// Holds the full value-type substrate and accessor surface for the v0.3
// compliant-joints-flexures PRD:
//   - `RotationalStiffness` alias placeholder
//   - `FlexureCompliance` structure_def (seven-field PRD §4.2 contract)
//   - `flexure_compliance(joint) -> FlexureCompliance` accessor
//
// The structure_def and accessor live in the same module, enabled by the
// skeleton pre-pass added in task 3895: `phase_functions` now builds
// transient `TopologyTemplate` skeletons from parsed same-module
// `structure_def`s so fn bodies can lower `Foo()` ctor expressions to
// `CompiledExprKind::StructureInstanceCtor` without waiting for
// `phase_entities` to run. This replaces the previous split into
// `std.flexures.types` + `std.flexures` (esc-3851-32 workaround — now
// retired).
//
// Module placement: registered in `stdlib_loader.rs` after `std.kinematic`
// (task #5476 — `structure def FlexureJoint : DrivingJoint` names the
// `DrivingJoint` trait, which is resolved through the loader's growing
// sequential prelude, so registration order IS visibility order). Depends on
// language primitives (Real, Pressure, Length, Bool) and the standard prelude
// units (Pa, N, m, etc.) from `std.units` and `std.si_units`. Zero
// cross-module dependencies on materials / structural / solver modules.
//
// Joint parameter typing (placeholder ERADICATED — task #5476,
// docs/prds/v0_6/placeholder-type-eradication-ratchet.md §3.2/§3.3):
//   `flexure_compliance(joint: FlexureJoint)` is nominally typed against the
//   `structure def FlexureJoint : DrivingJoint` marker declared below, and the
//   13 `prb_*` ctors type their call sites as `StructureRef("FlexureJoint")`
//   via `reify-compiler/src/flexure_signatures.rs`. The parameter was
//   PREVIOUSLY typed `Length` — a placeholder that let any bare `5mm`
//   overload-match and silently yield a sentinel-zero record. It no longer
//   does: a non-joint argument is now a hard compile Error carrying
//   `DiagnosticCode::NoMatchingOverload` (mnemonic `E_NO_MATCHING_OVERLOAD`).
//   The RUNTIME representation is unchanged — joints are still `Value::Map`,
//   and PRD §7.1's rejection of the joints→typed-StructureInstance migration
//   is NOT reopened. See the marker's own comment below for the full argument.
//
// Cache-on-joint convention (consumed by λ — task 3821):
//   The PRB ctors (γ / δ / ε / ζ / η / θ) will store the populated
//   `FlexureCompliance` record on the joint Map under the reserved hidden
//   field `__flexure_compliance` (mirroring KCC's `__joint_*` reserved-name
//   convention from task 3842 — `JointBinding::LiteralBound::synth_param_name
//   = __joint_<id>_v`). The accessor body in this module is stubbed to
//   return a default `FlexureCompliance()` until λ replaces the body with
//   the cache-lookup logic (or wires Rust-side intrinsic dispatch). The
//   reserved-name namespacing keeps the cache key discoverable and out of
//   user-namespace collision.

// ── Type aliases ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// RotationalStiffness — torsional spring constant relating angular
// displacement to restoring torque (N·m/rad² in SI, so that k·θ closes on
// Reify's N·m/rad torque; task #5799). PRD §4.2 names this as
// the canonical type for `FlexureCompliance.effective_stiffness`.
//
// No module-level alias is needed. Task 3849 landed `RotationalStiffness` (and
// the siblings `TranslationalStiffness` / `RotationalDamping` /
// `TranslationalDamping`) as built-in dimensioned types in `NAMED_DIMENSIONS`
// (reify-core dimension.rs). A `NAMED_DIMENSIONS` lookup takes priority over a
// module type-alias, so `effective_stiffness : RotationalStiffness` resolves to
// the built-in `DimensionVector::ROTATIONAL_STIFFNESS` directly — exactly as the
// three siblings are already used aliaslessly in kinematic.ri (Prismatic /
// Revolute `spring_rate` / `damping`).
//
// The former `pub type RotationalStiffness = Real` placeholder alias was
// therefore dead (shadowed by the built-in dimension) and is removed (task 4547,
// Disposition 2); it also carried a false claim that the dimensioned type "has
// not been filed yet" — 3849 filed and landed it.

// ── FlexureCompliance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// FlexureCompliance — the seven-field PRD §4.2 contract surfacing every
// flexure compliance metric a user (or downstream GUI panel) cares about
// when inspecting a flexure-emitted joint. The PRB ctors (γ / δ / ε / ζ /
// η / θ in PRD §11) populate one of these records per ctor call and cache
// it on the joint Map under the reserved hidden field
// `__flexure_compliance`; the `flexure_compliance(joint)` accessor below
// surfaces it.
//
// Field semantics (PRD §4.2):
//   effective_stiffness   — the equivalent linear-spring rate of the
//                           flexure-emitted joint (γ·E·I/L for a cantilever
//                           per Howell §5.2; analogous closed-form for each
//                           PRB primitive in PRD §5). Typed as the built-in
//                           `RotationalStiffness` dimension (N·m/rad²; task 3849
//                           NAMED_DIMENSIONS), so the field resolves to
//                           `DimensionVector::ROTATIONAL_STIFFNESS` directly.
//   max_stress            — peak bending stress at the declared range
//                           endpoint (PRD §5.1: σ_max = E·(t/2)·θ/L for a
//                           cantilever; analogous formula per PRB primitive).
//                           Pressure-typed because bending stress is force
//                           per unit area.
//   max_stress_at_neutral — peak stress at the joint's neutral position
//                           (rest). Zero unless preloaded (PRD §4.2);
//                           Pressure-typed for the same reason.
//   yield_margin          — dimensionless safety ratio
//                           `(material.yield_stress - max_stress) /
//                           material.yield_stress`. Bounded above by 1
//                           (max_stress = 0 → margin = 1); unbounded below
//                           (max_stress > yield → margin < 0 →
//                           `at_yield = true`). The structure-level
//                           constraint `yield_margin <= 1` encodes the
//                           upper bound as an explicit contract — see PRD
//                           §5.3 stress-check rationale.
//   parasitic_error       — orthogonal-DOF motion produced by the flexure
//                           at the declared range endpoint. Set on compound
//                           flexures (parallelogram, double-parallelogram —
//                           PRD §6.1, §6.2) where Roberts-approximation
//                           parasitic rotation is meaningful; `none` for
//                           single-blade flexures (PRD §5) where the
//                           parasitic-error notion doesn't apply.
//                           `Option<Length>` because the metric vanishes
//                           rather than going to zero for single-blade
//                           cases.
//   prb_validity_range    — range where the PRB approximation is accurate
//                           (~5% per PRD §5.1's `theta_prb_limit = 5deg`).
//                           Family-polymorphic at runtime: Range<Angle> for
//                           revolute/cantilever families, Range<Length> for
//                           prismatic/displacement families (task 4587).
//                           The declared type `Range<Angle>` is a nominal
//                           placeholder (paralleling `effective_stiffness`,
//                           which is declared `RotationalStiffness` but stores
//                           translational stiffness for prismatic families);
//                           records are built Rust-side with a placeholder
//                           type_id, so the declared type is not enforced on
//                           runtime values. Sentinel-zero default `0deg..0deg`
//                           matches the runtime `symmetric_angle_range(0.0)`
//                           posture. This type/value divergence is intentional
//                           tracked debt: a follow-up task should make the
//                           declared type family-polymorphic (or split the
//                           field) so the declared type matches the runtime
//                           value and future consumers (unit-aware
//                           introspection, serialisation, tightened ctor
//                           checks) cannot mis-handle prismatic records.
//   at_yield              — true iff `max_stress >= material.yield_stress`.
//                           PRB ctors emit `W_FlexureYielding` (PRD §5.3)
//                           when this flips true; the field stays on the
//                           record so user-side strict checks can read it
//                           without re-running the comparison.
//
// Defaults are deliberately "sentinel-zero" so the stub
// `flexure_compliance(joint)` accessor below — which currently returns a
// default `FlexureCompliance()` until λ wires real cache lookup — has a
// well-typed value to return. PRB ctors (γ / δ / ε / ζ / η / θ) override
// every field with computed values via named-field ctor syntax
// `FlexureCompliance(effective_stiffness: ..., max_stress: ..., ...)`, so
// the defaults never leak into populated records. Same posture as
// `solver_elastic.ri::ElasticOptions` (every default-bearing field
// overridable at call time).
//
// Task 4576: `prb_validity_range` tightened to `Range<Angle> = 0deg..0deg`
// (sentinel default); Range<Angle> resolver arm added to type_resolution.rs.
// Task 4587: prismatic/displacement families now emit Range<Length> at
// runtime; the declared `Range<Angle>` type is retained as a nominal
// placeholder (records are built Rust-side, bypassing the ctor type check).
structure def FlexureCompliance {
    param effective_stiffness : RotationalStiffness = 0N*m/rad^2
    param max_stress : Pressure = 0Pa
    param max_stress_at_neutral : Pressure = 0Pa
    param yield_margin : Real = 0.0
    param parasitic_error : Option<Length> = none
    param prb_validity_range : Range<Angle> = 0deg..0deg
    param at_yield : Bool = false

    // Defense-in-depth: `yield_margin = (yield - max_stress) / yield` is
    // mathematically bounded above by 1 (max attained at max_stress = 0).
    // It is unbounded below (becomes negative when max_stress > yield,
    // which is the "at_yield" regime per PRD §5.3 where PRB ctors emit
    // `W_FlexureYielding` and set `at_yield = true`). A populated record
    // with yield_margin > 1 indicates a PRB-ctor bug (e.g. swapped
    // numerator/denominator) — declaring the upper bound at the
    // structure-def level makes the contract explicit in production code
    // rather than relying solely on test coverage (task 2544 convention;
    // mirrors `BucklingOptions.n_modes > 0` in solver_buckling.ri and
    // `PiecewisePolynomialProfile.waypoints.count > 0` in
    // trajectory.ri).
    //
    // SIR-α's `check_constraints_against_templates` machinery (task 3540,
    // landed) evaluates structure-level constraints at the eval path, so
    // this fires at construction with no further plumbing in β.
    constraint yield_margin <= 1
}

// ── FlexureJoint marker ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// FlexureJoint — the nominal STATIC-LAYER marker for a joint produced by a
// PRB-emitting `prb_*` ctor. Placeholder-ratchet α, task #5476; PRD
// docs/prds/v0_6/placeholder-type-eradication-ratchet.md §3.1 / §3.2.
//
// Empty by design — it carries no fields, and that emptiness is load-bearing:
// the RUNTIME representation of a flexure joint is an untouched `Value::Map`
// (the `prb_*` ctors in `reify-stdlib/src/flexures/` are unchanged by α). PRD
// §7.1 keeps the joints→typed-StructureInstance migration REJECTED, exactly as
// KCC-ζ (task 3845, esc-3845-91) left it. Giving this marker fields would start
// that declined migration; it is a type-layer tag only. Precedent for the empty
// form: `structure def Spherical : DrivingJoint { }` in kinematic.ri.
//
// `: DrivingJoint` so the #4310 compile-time bound checks on `bind` / `sweep` /
// `dim` treat a flexure joint uniformly with every other driving joint — to the
// mechanism layer "it is just a Revolute". Without the bound, retyping the
// `prb_*` ctors would make previously-accepted `bind(prb_notch_circular(…), …)`
// call sites fail the bound check.
//
// Non-matchability comes free from the compiler's EXACT-EQUALITY overload
// filter (`type_compat.rs::resolve_function_overload`): once
// `flexure_compliance` declares `joint: FlexureJoint` (step-12), a bare `5mm`
// is `Scalar[LENGTH]`, does not equal `StructureRef("FlexureJoint")`, and is
// rejected as a hard compile Error. No new `Type` variant and no new grammar
// are required (PRD §3.1) — this is a plain `structure def`.
//
// `pub`-visible by necessity, not by preference: it names the parameter type in
// `flexure_compliance`'s public signature, so user code must be able to spell
// it. That makes a bare `FlexureJoint()` fabrication statically legal — a
// deliberate residual hole. It stays DYNAMICALLY loud via the eval-time
// `W_FLEXURE_NON_JOINT_ARG` classifier (task 4547 Disposition 5), which is
// RETAINED as defense-in-depth precisely for this case (PRD §3.6): a fabricated
// marker carries no `__flexure_compliance` cache entry, so the classifier still
// fires. Statically-silent AND dynamically-silent is what INV-SF-5 bans.
structure def FlexureJoint : DrivingJoint { }

// ── flexure_compliance accessor ───────────────────────────────────────────

// flexure_compliance(joint) — the PRD §4.2 FREE-function accessor that
// surfaces the cached `FlexureCompliance` record from a joint produced by a
// PRB-emitting ctor (γ / δ / ε / ζ / η / θ in PRD §11).
//
// FREE-function spelling (not method syntax) — Reify grammar has no
// `x.foo()` method-call form for user-defined free functions; per-kind
// `Joint` structures don't exist yet either (KCC-ζ owns that surface, task
// 3845 in-progress). Matches the PRD §4.2 prescribed spelling
// `flexure_compliance(joint) -> FlexureCompliance`.
//
// Stub semantics (β — this task):
//   The body returns `FlexureCompliance()` — a bare ctor call relying on
//   every param's sentinel-zero default. The same-module skeleton pre-pass
//   (task 3895, functions_phase.rs) makes `FlexureCompliance` visible to
//   this fn body without needing a separate prelude module. PRB ctors
//   override every field via named-field ctor syntax
//   `FlexureCompliance(effective_stiffness: ..., max_stress: ..., ...)`,
//   so the defaults never leak into populated records once γ / δ / ε / ζ /
//   η / θ land. Exact precedent: the bare `Steel_AISI_1045()`
//   instantiation pattern in `materials_fea.ri` (every field
//   defaulted at the structure_def level, ctor call is bare).
//
// `pub` so user code can call `flexure_compliance(some_joint)` directly
// without prefixing with the `std.flexures::` module path — same posture
// as `pub fn STANDARD_GRAVITY()` in `units.ri` and `pub fn
// standard_bolt_lengths()` in `standard_stock.ri`.
//
// Joint parameter typing — the `Length` placeholder is GONE (task #5476,
// docs/prds/v0_6/placeholder-type-eradication-ratchet.md §3.2/§3.3/§3.6):
//
// `joint` is NOMINALLY typed as the `structure def FlexureJoint : DrivingJoint`
// marker declared above. The 13 `prb_*` flexure constructors type their call
// sites as `StructureRef("FlexureJoint")` (compiler-side signature family:
// `reify-compiler/src/flexure_signatures.rs`), so a real PRB-ctor result
// overload-matches and nothing else does.
//
// What this changed, concretely: the parameter used to be typed `Length` purely
// so the accessor would overload-match a PRB-ctor result, because with no
// signature arm the compiler inferred a bare-builtin call's return type from its
// first `Length` argument. That made ANY `Length` — a bare `5mm` — match too,
// yielding the sentinel-default record instead of a type error. A non-joint
// argument was statically indistinguishable from a real joint. It no longer is:
// the compiler's EXACT-EQUALITY overload filter
// (`type_compat.rs::resolve_function_overload`) sees `Scalar[LENGTH]` !=
// `StructureRef("FlexureJoint")` and emits a hard compile Error carrying
// `DiagnosticCode::NoMatchingOverload` (PRD-prose mnemonic
// `E_NO_MATCHING_OVERLOAD`). Boundary tests: PRD §7.3 BT1/BT2 —
// `reify-cli/tests/harness_cli/cli_check.rs` (the user-observable exit-code
// flip) and `reify-compiler/tests/harness_mechanics/flexures_stdlib_compile.rs`
// (the typed code).
//
// What this did NOT change: the RUNTIME representation. Joints are still
// `Value::Map` per `reify-stdlib/src/joints.rs`. PRD §7.1's rejection of the
// joints→typed-StructureInstance migration stands and is NOT reopened here —
// `FlexureJoint` is a static-layer nominal tag over an untouched runtime value,
// which is why no new `Type` variant and no grammar change were needed.
//
// Residual hole, deliberately left open and deliberately covered: because
// `FlexureJoint` must be spellable in this public signature, a bare
// `FlexureJoint()` fabrication is statically legal. The eval-time
// `W_FLEXURE_NON_JOINT_ARG` classifier (task 4547 Disposition 5,
// `reify-stdlib/src/flexures/diagnostics.rs`) is therefore RETAINED as
// defense-in-depth, NOT superseded (PRD §3.6): a fabricated marker carries no
// `__flexure_compliance` cache entry, so the classifier still fires at eval
// time. Statically-silent AND dynamically-silent is what INV-SF-5 bans; keeping
// both arms is what makes this a ratchet rather than a swap.
pub fn flexure_compliance(joint: FlexureJoint) -> FlexureCompliance {
    __flexure_compliance_get(joint)
}
